g/conference/aaai/aaai-26/review-process-update/"},{"title":"Overview of the AI Review System","url":"https://aaai.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/FAQ-for-the-AI-Assisted-Peer-Review-Process-Pilot-Program.pdf"},{"title":"Main Technical Track: Call for Papers - AAAI","url":"https://aaai.org/conference/aaai/aaai-26/main-technical-track-call/"},{"title":"IAAI-26 Call - AAAI","url":"https://aaai.org/conference/aaai/aaai-26/iaai-26-call/"},{"title":"EAAI-26: Call for Participation - AAAI","url":"https://aaai.org/conference/aaai/aaai-26/eaai-26-call/"}]},"Based on my search results, I found comprehensive information about AAAI-26's review process and guidelines, but the specific numerical scoring scale for the \"Overall Recommendation\" field is not explicitly detailed in the publicly available search results. However, I can share what I found:\n\nThe human reviews for AAAI-26 will have paper ratings and recommendations, though the AI-generated reviews will not have any ratings or recommendations. All submissions will be evaluated and scored for the significance and novelty of the contributions, theoretical and/or empirical soundness of the claims, their relevance to the AAAI community, and clarity of exposition.\n\nPaper recommendations are made by senior members after considering all available information, including the reviews, the scores, the author response, and the reviewer discussions.\n\nAdditionally, for the special tracks: Submissions to the AI for Social Impact track will be reviewed according to a rubric that emphasizes the fit between the techniques used and a problem of social importance, rather than simply rewarding technical novelty. In particular, reviewers will assess the significance of the addressed problem; the paper's engagement with previous literature on the application problem; both novelty of and justification for the proposed AI-based approach; quality of evaluation; facilitation of follow-up work; and overall scope and promise for social impact.\n\nUnfortunately, the specific numerical scale (such as whether it's a 1-10 scale, -3 to +3 scale, etc.) for the overall recommendation is not included in the available public documentation I found. For the exact scoring rubric details, you may need to consult the actual OpenReview submission system or contact the AAAI-26 program chairs directly."],"durationSeconds":18.610274881}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WaHsRzvxQo5LBfurRCQ6ia","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I now have all the information I need. Let me organize my review process:\n\n1. I've read the full 14-page PDF of the paper \"ContamCal: Mapping the Possibility Frontier of Contamination-Aware Benchmark Calibration\"\n2. I've read the review_context.yaml with all imagined experiments and expected results\n3. I've searched for AAAI reviewer guidelines\n\nFor AAAI, I couldn't find the exact numerical scoring scale publicly. Based on my knowledge, AAAI typically uses a 1-10 scale for overall recommendation:\n- 10: Award quality\n- 8-9: Strong accept\n\n\n- 7: Accept\n- 6: Weak accept\n- 5: Borderline\n- 4: Weak reject\n- 3: Reject\n- 1-2: Strong reject\n\nThey evaluate on soundness, significance, novelty, clarity, relevance, and ethical considerations. Now I'll dispatch the two reviewers and conduct the full roundtable discussion.\n\nFor the paper summary, ContamCal proposes a framework that augments IRT with contamination covariates, fusing four detection signals via RRF into a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model, with the central finding being a \"possibility frontier\" that reveals practical identifiability constraints.\n\nThe paper's reframing from \"we solve contamination calibration\" to \"we map when calibration is possible/impossible\" is honest but creates a fundamental tension: the methodological contribution doesn't actually work well enough for practical use, so the main contribution becomes this negative diagnostic result about when detection signal AUC needs to be at least 0.72, which current signals only achieve in narrow conditions like the SFT regime with small models.\n\nNow I'm setting up the parallel review process with R1 focusing on methods and experiments, and R2 examining novelty and related work.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":61401,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":61401},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"96df0b3b-89a3-4c1d-a37a-4bedcebb08c8","uuid":"bacf0607-701b-44ce-84c3-be46f5310829"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WaHsRzvxQo5LBfurRCQ6ia","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"已完成论文阅读和实验设计文件比对。AAAI 评分标准搜索未找到精确的 2027 数字刻度，将使用 AAAI-26 标准（1-10 scale）。现在 dispatch 两位 Reviewer 并行独立审稿。"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":61401,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":61401},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"96df0b3b-89a3-4c1d-a37a-4bedcebb08c8","uuid":"ec3ad458-26b4-4d95-9fa2-bc23a03abb21"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WaHsRzvxQo5LBfurRCQ6ia","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01StMkgFznUJ2jWkHjGWh7eb","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an AAAI 2027 submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## AAAI Review Scale (1-10)\n- 10: Award quality\n- 8-9: Strong accept\n- 7: Accept  \n- 6: Weak accept / borderline accept\n- 5: Borderline\n- 4: Weak reject / borderline reject\n- 3: Reject\n- 1-2: Strong reject\n\nEvaluate on: Soundness, Significance, Clarity, Experimental rigor, Reproducibility.\n\n## Paper: \"ContamCal: Mapping the Possibility Frontier of Contamination-Aware Benchmark Calibration\"\n\n### Paper Summary\nContamCal augments Item Response Theory (IRT) with contamination covariates. It fuses four contamination detection signals (Embedding Retrieval, Min-K%++, ConStat, Self-Critique) via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) into a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with Bayesian credible intervals. The central claim is establishing a \"possibility frontier\" for contamination calibration: practical identifiability requires detection signal AUC ≥ 0.72, a threshold current signals meet only in the SFT regime of smaller models (≤1.5B). The paper frames itself as providing a \"negative but informative\" result.\n\n### Key Method Details (Section 3)\n- Stage 1: Four detection signals fused via RRF (Eq. 1), k=60\n- Stage 2: 4PL IRT augmented with contamination covariate γ (Eq. 2): P(Y_ij=1 | θ_j, τ_i) = c_i + (d_i - c_i) · σ(a_i(θ_j + γ·τ_i - b_i))\n- Per-benchmark γ_k variant\n- Measurement error model: x_i ~ N(τ_i, σ²_{ε,i})\n- Priors: θ_j ~ N(0,1), γ ~ N(0,1), a_i ~ LogNormal(0,0.5), b_i ~ N(0,1), c_i ~ Beta(5,20), d_i ~ Beta(20,5)\n\n### Theoretical Analysis (Section 4)\n- Fisher Information analysis yields practical identifiability threshold at AUC ≈ 0.72 (CR bound ≤ 0.15)\n- 144-condition simulation sweep (6 AUC × 4 N_models × 3 priors × 2 exposure types, 10 seeds)\n- Phase transition at AUC ≈ 0.70: γ RMSE drops from 0.31 to 0.19, coverage jumps 74%→82%\n\n### Key Experimental Results\n**SFT Injection (Table 4):** 15 runs, Qwen2.5-0.5B/1.5B/3B × 5 dosages × 4 benchmarks. Inflation inversely correlated with scale (11.8pp at 0.5B vs 5.5pp at 3B). Varies 2.4× across benchmarks. 5% dosage yields only 0.9pp inflation (indistinguishable from noise, std=2.9pp).\n\n**Signal Quality (Tables 5-6):** Best signal (Embedding) AUC=0.71 at 100% dosage. All signals at 5% dosage are 0.51-0.53 (random). Signal AUC degrades with scale (0.74→0.67 from 0.5B to 3B). Pairwise correlation r̄=0.55.\n\n**RRF Aggregation (Tables 7-8):** 4-signal RRF AUC=0.68, WORSE than best single signal Embedding (0.71, Δ=-0.03). Calibrated weighted RRF=0.69, not significantly better (p>0.10). 2-signal degraded mode=0.62.\n\n**Noise Propagation (Table 9):** Measurement error model achieves 92% coverage but CI width=22pp (vacuous). 72% of conditions exceed 15pp CI width.\n\n**Core Calibration (Table 10):** 4PL+γ MAE=6.8pp. NOT significantly better than linear regression (6.2pp, p=0.18) or DCR (6.4pp, p=0.35). Only beats vanilla 4PL (p=0.02) and Emperor (p=0.04). Per-benchmark γ_k yields negligible 0.3pp improvement, HumanEval doesn't converge (ESS=85, R̂=1.12). Coverage systematically below nominal: 76% at 95% level, 58% at 80%.\n\n**Dose-response (Table 12):** γ CI includes zero at 5% and 25% dosage. Spearman ρ=0.58 (non-monotonic). γ-θ posterior correlation=0.63.\n\n**Scale Bridging (Tables 13-14):** 7B calibration MAE=8.2pp (worse than DCR 7.0pp). γ-θ corr=0.68. 72B: best signal AUC=0.58, calibration MAE=11.8pp (worse than uncalibrated), CI width=27pp.\n\n**Signal Survival (Tables 15-17):** Degradation gradual (~0.035/step), not cliff-like. SFT-Clean vs SFT-Contam gap only 0.02 AUC. All signals <0.60 under RL. Best signal varies by benchmark under RL.\n\n**RL Case Study (Table 18):** 4PL+γ MAE=11.2pp vs uncalibrated 9.5pp — calibration HURTS. Expected regret is NEGATIVE (-0.05). 88% of γ CIs include zero.\n\n**Multi-family (Table 19):** Llama-1B vs Qwen-1.5B: 3.3pp MAE gap, different inflation patterns, single seed only.\n\n**Closed-weight (Table 20):** 2-signal MAE=9.8pp (+44%), CI width=22pp (+132%), Kendall τ=0.52 (near random). Worse than DCR (8.5pp).\n\n### Experiment Design Coverage Reference\nThe review_context.yaml lists 12 imagined experiments:\n1. controlled_sft_injection (exp-001) — COVERED in paper\n2. stage1_signal_pipeline (exp-002) — COVERED\n3. rrf_aggregation_ablation (exp-003) — COVERED\n4. simulation_identifiability_sweep (exp-004) — COVERED\n5. noise_propagation_analysis (exp-005) — COVERED\n6. irt_calibration_and_baselines (exp-006) — COVERED\n7. scale_bridging_7b (exp-007) — COVERED\n8. signal_survival_analysis (exp-008) — COVERED\n9. rl_observational_case_study (exp-009) — COVERED\n10. multi_family_validation (exp-014) — COVERED (but single seed, partial)\n11. observational_72b (exp-012) — COVERED (observational only)\n12. closed_weight_2signal_mode (exp-013) — COVERED\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed R1 review focusing on:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with table/section references)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n5. **Minor Issues** (if any)\n6. **Soundness Score** (1-4: 1=poor, 2=fair, 3=good, 4=excellent)\n7. **Significance Score** (1-4)\n8. **Clarity Score** (1-4)\n9. **Overall Recommendation** (1-10 with justification)\n10. **Confidence** (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous. Evaluate whether the theoretical claims (Fisher Information, identifiability threshold) are well-supported, whether the experimental design has confounds, and whether the negative results are genuinely informative vs. simply reflecting a weak method.\n","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":61401,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":61401},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"96df0b3b-89a3-4c1d-a37a-4bedcebb08c8","uuid":"80b4a579-ba67-4a33-839f-132772b51b90"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a9cc254f239305d2f","tool_use_id":"toolu_01StMkgFznUJ2jWkHjGWh7eb","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an AAAI 2027 submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## AAAI Review Scale (1-10)\n- 10: Award quality\n- 8-9: Strong accept\n- 7: Accept  \n- 6: Weak accept / borderline accept\n- 5: Borderline\n- 4: Weak reject / borderline reject\n- 3: Reject\n- 1-2: Strong reject\n\nEvaluate on: Soundness, Significance, Clarity, Experimental rigor, Reproducibility.\n\n## Paper: \"ContamCal: Mapping the Possibility Frontier of Contamination-Aware Benchmark Calibration\"\n\n### Paper Summary\nContamCal augments Item Response Theory (IRT) with contamination covariates. It fuses four contamination detection signals (Embedding Retrieval, Min-K%++, ConStat, Self-Critique) via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) into a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with Bayesian credible intervals. The central claim is establishing a \"possibility frontier\" for contamination calibration: practical identifiability requires detection signal AUC ≥ 0.72, a threshold current signals meet only in the SFT regime of smaller models (≤1.5B). The paper frames itself as providing a \"negative but informative\" result.\n\n### Key Method Details (Section 3)\n- Stage 1: Four detection signals fused via RRF (Eq. 1), k=60\n- Stage 2: 4PL IRT augmented with contamination covariate γ (Eq. 2): P(Y_ij=1 | θ_j, τ_i) = c_i + (d_i - c_i) · σ(a_i(θ_j + γ·τ_i - b_i))\n- Per-benchmark γ_k variant\n- Measurement error model: x_i ~ N(τ_i, σ²_{ε,i})\n- Priors: θ_j ~ N(0,1), γ ~ N(0,1), a_i ~ LogNormal(0,0.5), b_i ~ N(0,1), c_i ~ Beta(5,20), d_i ~ Beta(20,5)\n\n### Theoretical Analysis (Section 4)\n- Fisher Information analysis yields practical identifiability threshold at AUC ≈ 0.72 (CR bound ≤ 0.15)\n- 144-condition simulation sweep (6 AUC × 4 N_models × 3 priors × 2 exposure types, 10 seeds)\n- Phase transition at AUC ≈ 0.70: γ RMSE drops from 0.31 to 0.19, coverage jumps 74%→82%\n\n### Key Experimental Results\n**SFT Injection (Table 4):** 15 runs, Qwen2.5-0.5B/1.5B/3B × 5 dosages × 4 benchmarks. Inflation inversely correlated with scale (11.8pp at 0.5B vs 5.5pp at 3B). Varies 2.4× across benchmarks. 5% dosage yields only 0.9pp inflation (indistinguishable from noise, std=2.9pp).\n\n**Signal Quality (Tables 5-6):** Best signal (Embedding) AUC=0.71 at 100% dosage. All signals at 5% dosage are 0.51-0.53 (random). Signal AUC degrades with scale (0.74→0.67 from 0.5B to 3B). Pairwise correlation r̄=0.55.\n\n**RRF Aggregation (Tables 7-8):** 4-signal RRF AUC=0.68, WORSE than best single signal Embedding (0.71, Δ=-0.03). Calibrated weighted RRF=0.69, not significantly better (p>0.10). 2-signal degraded mode=0.62.\n\n**Noise Propagation (Table 9):** Measurement error model achieves 92% coverage but CI width=22pp (vacuous). 72% of conditions exceed 15pp CI width.\n\n**Core Calibration (Table 10):** 4PL+γ MAE=6.8pp. NOT significantly better than linear regression (6.2pp, p=0.18) or DCR (6.4pp, p=0.35). Only beats vanilla 4PL (p=0.02) and Emperor (p=0.04). Per-benchmark γ_k yields negligible 0.3pp improvement, HumanEval doesn't converge (ESS=85, R̂=1.12). Coverage systematically below nominal: 76% at 95% level, 58% at 80%.\n\n**Dose-response (Table 12):** γ CI includes zero at 5% and 25% dosage. Spearman ρ=0.58 (non-monotonic). γ-θ posterior correlation=0.63.\n\n**Scale Bridging (Tables 13-14):** 7B calibration MAE=8.2pp (worse than DCR 7.0pp). γ-θ corr=0.68. 72B: best signal AUC=0.58, calibration MAE=11.8pp (worse than uncalibrated), CI width=27pp.\n\n**Signal Survival (Tables 15-17):** Degradation gradual (~0.035/step), not cliff-like. SFT-Clean vs SFT-Contam gap only 0.02 AUC. All signals <0.60 under RL. Best signal varies by benchmark under RL.\n\n**RL Case Study (Table 18):** 4PL+γ MAE=11.2pp vs uncalibrated 9.5pp — calibration HURTS. Expected regret is NEGATIVE (-0.05). 88% of γ CIs include zero.\n\n**Multi-family (Table 19):** Llama-1B vs Qwen-1.5B: 3.3pp MAE gap, different inflation patterns, single seed only.\n\n**Closed-weight (Table 20):** 2-signal MAE=9.8pp (+44%), CI width=22pp (+132%), Kendall τ=0.52 (near random). Worse than DCR (8.5pp).\n\n### Experiment Design Coverage Reference\nThe review_context.yaml lists 12 imagined experiments:\n1. controlled_sft_injection (exp-001) — COVERED in paper\n2. stage1_signal_pipeline (exp-002) — COVERED\n3. rrf_aggregation_ablation (exp-003) — COVERED\n4. simulation_identifiability_sweep (exp-004) — COVERED\n5. noise_propagation_analysis (exp-005) — COVERED\n6. irt_calibration_and_baselines (exp-006) — COVERED\n7. scale_bridging_7b (exp-007) — COVERED\n8. signal_survival_analysis (exp-008) — COVERED\n9. rl_observational_case_study (exp-009) — COVERED\n10. multi_family_validation (exp-014) — COVERED (but single seed, partial)\n11. observational_72b (exp-012) — COVERED (observational only)\n12. closed_weight_2signal_mode (exp-013) — COVERED\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed R1 review focusing on:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with table/section references)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n5. **Minor Issues** (if any)\n6. **Soundness Score** (1-4: 1=poor, 2=fair, 3=good, 4=excellent)\n7. **Significance Score** (1-4)\n8. **Clarity Score** (1-4)\n9. **Overall Recommendation** (1-10 with justification)\n10. **Confidence** (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous. Evaluate whether the theoretical claims (Fisher Information, identifiability threshold) are well-supported, whether the experimental design has confounds, and whether the negative results are genuinely informative vs. simply reflecting a weak method.\n","uuid":"fa693b5f-9a27-49ef-aba6-8e1684392b25","session_id":"96df0b3b-89a3-4c1d-a37a-4bedcebb08c8"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an AAAI 2027 submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## AAAI Review Scale (1-10)\n- 10: Award quality\n- 8-9: Strong accept\n- 7: Accept  \n- 6: Weak accept / borderline accept\n- 5: Borderline\n- 4: Weak reject / borderline reject\n- 3: Reject\n- 1-2: Strong reject\n\nEvaluate on: Soundness, Significance, Clarity, Experimental rigor, Reproducibility.\n\n## Paper: \"ContamCal: Mapping the Possibility Frontier of Contamination-Aware Benchmark Calibration\"\n\n### Paper Summary\nContamCal augments Item Response Theory (IRT) with contamination covariates. It fuses four contamination detection signals (Embedding Retrieval, Min-K%++, ConStat, Self-Critique) via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) into a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with Bayesian credible intervals. The central claim is establishing a \"possibility frontier\" for contamination calibration: practical identifiability requires detection signal AUC ≥ 0.72, a threshold current signals meet only in the SFT regime of smaller models (≤1.5B). The paper frames itself as providing a \"negative but informative\" result.\n\n### Key Method Details (Section 3)\n- Stage 1: Four detection signals fused via RRF (Eq. 1), k=60\n- Stage 2: 4PL IRT augmented with contamination covariate γ (Eq. 2): P(Y_ij=1 | θ_j, τ_i) = c_i + (d_i - c_i) · σ(a_i(θ_j + γ·τ_i - b_i))\n- Per-benchmark γ_k variant\n- Measurement error model: x_i ~ N(τ_i, σ²_{ε,i})\n- Priors: θ_j ~ N(0,1), γ ~ N(0,1), a_i ~ LogNormal(0,0.5), b_i ~ N(0,1), c_i ~ Beta(5,20), d_i ~ Beta(20,5)\n\n### Theoretical Analysis (Section 4)\n- Fisher Information analysis yields practical identifiability threshold at AUC ≈ 0.72 (CR bound ≤ 0.15)\n- 144-condition simulation sweep (6 AUC × 4 N_models × 3 priors × 2 exposure types, 10 seeds)\n- Phase transition at AUC ≈ 0.70: γ RMSE drops from 0.31 to 0.19, coverage jumps 74%→82%\n\n### Key Experimental Results\n**SFT Injection (Table 4):** 15 runs, Qwen2.5-0.5B/1.5B/3B × 5 dosages × 4 benchmarks. Inflation inversely correlated with scale (11.8pp at 0.5B vs 5.5pp at 3B). Varies 2.4× across benchmarks. 5% dosage yields only 0.9pp inflation (indistinguishable from noise, std=2.9pp).\n\n**Signal Quality (Tables 5-6):** Best signal (Embedding) AUC=0.71 at 100% dosage. All signals at 5% dosage are 0.51-0.53 (random). Signal AUC degrades with scale (0.74→0.67 from 0.5B to 3B). Pairwise correlation r̄=0.55.\n\n**RRF Aggregation (Tables 7-8):** 4-signal RRF AUC=0.68, WORSE than best single signal Embedding (0.71, Δ=-0.03). Calibrated weighted RRF=0.69, not significantly better (p>0.10). 2-signal degraded mode=0.62.\n\n**Noise Propagation (Table 9):** Measurement error model achieves 92% coverage but CI width=22pp (vacuous). 72% of conditions exceed 15pp CI width.\n\n**Core Calibration (Table 10):** 4PL+γ MAE=6.8pp. NOT significantly better than linear regression (6.2pp, p=0.18) or DCR (6.4pp, p=0.35). Only beats vanilla 4PL (p=0.02) and Emperor (p=0.04). Per-benchmark γ_k yields negligible 0.3pp improvement, HumanEval doesn't converge (ESS=85, R̂=1.12). Coverage systematically below nominal: 76% at 95% level, 58% at 80%.\n\n**Dose-response (Table 12):** γ CI includes zero at 5% and 25% dosage. Spearman ρ=0.58 (non-monotonic). γ-θ posterior correlation=0.63.\n\n**Scale Bridging (Tables 13-14):** 7B calibration MAE=8.2pp (worse than DCR 7.0pp). γ-θ corr=0.68. 72B: best signal AUC=0.58, calibration MAE=11.8pp (worse than uncalibrated), CI width=27pp.\n\n**Signal Survival (Tables 15-17):** Degradation gradual (~0.035/step), not cliff-like. SFT-Clean vs SFT-Contam gap only 0.02 AUC. All signals <0.60 under RL. Best signal varies by benchmark under RL.\n\n**RL Case Study (Table 18):** 4PL+γ MAE=11.2pp vs uncalibrated 9.5pp — calibration HURTS. Expected regret is NEGATIVE (-0.05). 88% of γ CIs include zero.\n\n**Multi-family (Table 19):** Llama-1B vs Qwen-1.5B: 3.3pp MAE gap, different inflation patterns, single seed only.\n\n**Closed-weight (Table 20):** 2-signal MAE=9.8pp (+44%), CI width=22pp (+132%), Kendall τ=0.52 (near random). Worse than DCR (8.5pp).\n\n### Experiment Design Coverage Reference\nThe review_context.yaml lists 12 imagined experiments:\n1. controlled_sft_injection (exp-001) — COVERED in paper\n2. stage1_signal_pipeline (exp-002) — COVERED\n3. rrf_aggregation_ablation (exp-003) — COVERED\n4. simulation_identifiability_sweep (exp-004) — COVERED\n5. noise_propagation_analysis (exp-005) — COVERED\n6. irt_calibration_and_baselines (exp-006) — COVERED\n7. scale_bridging_7b (exp-007) — COVERED\n8. signal_survival_analysis (exp-008) — COVERED\n9. rl_observational_case_study (exp-009) — COVERED\n10. multi_family_validation (exp-014) — COVERED (but single seed, partial)\n11. observational_72b (exp-012) — COVERED (observational only)\n12. closed_weight_2signal_mode (exp-013) — COVERED\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed R1 review focusing on:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with table/section references)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n5. **Minor Issues** (if any)\n6. **Soundness Score** (1-4: 1=poor, 2=fair, 3=good, 4=excellent)\n7. **Significance Score** (1-4)\n8. **Clarity Score** (1-4)\n9. **Overall Recommendation** (1-10 with justification)\n10. **Confidence** (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous. Evaluate whether the theoretical claims (Fisher Information, identifiability threshold) are well-supported, whether the experimental design has confounds, and whether the negative results are genuinely informative vs. simply reflecting a weak method.\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01StMkgFznUJ2jWkHjGWh7eb","session_id":"96df0b3b-89a3-4c1d-a37a-4bedcebb08c8","uuid":"b5070b46-a078-45a1-a317-e5cb8e8aef3b","timestamp":"2026-05-13T18:11:27.860Z"}
