{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019ad5M7EPwwvs8XHXfpAx7T","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the materials needed. Let me synthesize my review. I've examined three versions of the paper (best, median, worst dream reports), the actual paper draft (introduction, related work, method sections), the paper framing document, the project roadmap, and the decisions log. I notice a critical discrepancy: the paper summary provided in the prompt describes a \"negative result\" paper (the \"worst\" dream tier), while the actual paper draft and \"best\" dream report describe a paper where ContamCal works well (1.5pp MAE, 93% coverage). I will review the version described in the prompt -- the \"possibility frontier\" / negative-result framing -- as that is what was assigned.\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: ContamCal -- Novelty and Related Work Focus\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper proposes ContamCal, a two-stage framework that augments 4PL Item Response Theory with contamination covariates derived from fusing four detection signals via Reciprocal Rank Fusion. The central finding is a \"possibility frontier\": Fisher Information analysis yields an AUC threshold of 0.72, below which the contamination parameter becomes unidentifiable, and current detection signals meet this threshold only under narrow conditions (SFT regime, small models). The paper frames this as a \"negative but informative\" result, arguing that it characterizes impossibility regions for any post-hoc calibration method.\n\n### 2. Novelty Assessment\n\n**Genuinely new:**\n- The idea of formally characterizing identifiability bounds for contamination calibration via Fisher Information analysis is novel. No prior work has asked \"under what signal quality conditions is contamination calibration even feasible?\" This is a legitimate and useful question.\n- The signal survival heatmap (signal x regime AUC matrix) is empirically novel. While Wang et al. (2025) showed RL degrades detection signals generally, the systematic four-signal x four-regime matrix with cliff-vs-gradual characterization adds value.\n\n**Incremental:**\n- The 4PL+gamma IRT model is a direct application of item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) with continuous rather than binary exposure covariates. The three \"structural adaptations\" are presented as non-trivial, but (a) fitting IRT to non-independent examinees has been discussed extensively in the psychometric literature (Reise & Waller 2009, Embretson & Reise 2000), (b) continuous covariates are standard in explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004), and (c) \"regime-conditional ability\" is handled through the measurement error variance rather than through actual regime-specific parameters, making it a notational distinction rather than a modeling advance.\n- RRF fusion of detection signals is explicitly disclaimed as a contribution, yet it occupies substantial space in the method section. This is honest but raises the question of what the actual methodological contribution is beyond the IRT application.\n- The measurement error model (Eq. 3, treating observed exposure as noisy proxy for latent exposure) is standard Bayesian measurement error modeling -- not novel, though its application to this domain is sensible.\n\n**Known:**\n- The causal inference analogy (measuring and adjusting for confounders rather than eliminating them) has been invoked in numerous applied statistics papers and does not constitute novelty.\n- The finding that detection signals degrade under RL post-training is already documented by Wang et al. (2025) and Wust et al. (2025). The contribution here is the quantitative characterization, not the phenomenon.\n- The \"mitigation doesn't work\" motivation is entirely from Xu et al. (2025), cited appropriately but meaning the problem motivation is derivative.\n\n### 3. Related Work Gaps\n\n**Missing references (significant):**\n- **Membership inference literature beyond Min-K%++:** Carlini et al. (2021, \"Extracting Training Data from Large Language Models\"), Mattern et al. (2023), and Shi et al. (2024, \"Detecting Pretraining Data from Large Language Models\") are foundational membership inference works that should be cited. The paper cites \"Shi et al. 2024\" in the prompt summary but uses \"Duan et al. 2025\" and \"Zhang et al. 2024\" in different draft versions -- this inconsistency raises concerns about citation management.\n- **Benchmark contamination surveys:** Deng et al. (2024, \"Investigating Data Contamination in Modern Benchmarks for Large Language Models\"), Balloccu et al. (2024) appear in some draft versions but not all. A comprehensive survey citation is essential.\n- **IRT identifiability literature:** San Martin et al. (2015) on identifiability of multidimensional IRT, Maris & Bechger (2009) on 3PL identifiability -- the paper cites these but does not engage deeply with the identifiability theory that underpins the Fisher Information analysis. How does the Fisher Information bound relate to the known identifiability pathologies of 3PL/4PL models (e.g., the Maris & Bechger argument that 3PL is not identifiable without constraints)?\n- **Reise & Waller (2009)** on IRT applications outside educational measurement -- directly relevant to the \"independent examinees\" adaptation claim.\n- **Embretson & Reise (2000)** \"Item Response Theory for Psychologists\" -- foundational reference missing.\n- **Causal inference for measurement:** The paper draws an analogy to causal inference but cites only Rosenbaum & Rubin (1983). If this analogy is central to the framing, Pearl (2009), Hernan & Robins (2020), or at minimum Rubin (1974) should be engaged with. As currently presented, the analogy is gestural rather than formal.\n- **Contamination in other evaluation paradigms:** Oren et al. (2024, \"Proving Test Set Contamination in Black Box Language Models\") -- cited inconsistently across drafts. Jacovi et al. (2023), Li et al. (2024) on contamination in generation benchmarks.\n- **Recent IRT for LLMs:** Gu et al. (2026, PSN-IRT) and Zhuang et al. (2025, HiBayES) appear in the actual draft but not in the prompt's reference list of 22 citations. The actual draft has approximately 23 references, which remains thin for a venue like AAAI/EMNLP that typically expects 30-50 citations.\n\n**Unfair comparisons / positioning issues:**\n- The positioning table (Table 1 in the related work draft) marks ContamCal as having all four desirable properties (Detects, Calibrates, UQ, RL-robust) with checkmarks. But the paper's own results show that ContamCal's RL-robustness consists of producing wide, uninformative CIs. Claiming \"RL-robust\" when the method produces 11.8pp MAE (worse than uncalibrated) under RL is misleading. \"RL-honest\" would be more accurate, but that is a different property than \"RL-robust.\"\n- DCR is characterized as lacking \"uncertainty bounds and RL handling,\" but DCR was not designed for these settings. The comparison should acknowledge scope differences rather than treating missing features as deficiencies.\n- Self-Critique is used as a signal throughout the paper but the citation situation is unclear. Different drafts cite it as \"Wust et al. 2025\" or \"Wentzel et al. 2024\" -- which is it? The arXiv ID \"2406.12345\" in the best-case draft bibliography appears suspiciously round and may be fabricated.\n\n### 4. Strengths\n\n- **Intellectually honest framing of limitations.** The paper acknowledges that its method does not work under RL and at large scales, and frames this as an identifiability boundary rather than hiding it. This is uncommon and commendable in ML papers.\n- **Well-grounded in psychometric theory.** The connection to item compromise IRT (Eckerly, Sinharay) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson) is appropriate and demonstrates interdisciplinary sophistication. The three structural adaptations, while incremental, are correctly identified.\n- **Fisher Information analysis provides theoretical grounding.** Even if the AUC threshold is ultimately a simple calculation, formalizing the identifiability boundary is a genuine contribution that gives the community a concrete target for detection signal improvement.\n- **Measurement error model is principled.** Propagating Stage 1 uncertainty into Stage 2 posteriors is the correct Bayesian approach, and the coverage comparison (83% without vs. 93% with) demonstrates its importance.\n- **Signal survival analysis is empirically valuable.** The signal x regime heatmap, even if the finding (RL degrades signals) is partially known, provides the first systematic quantification with a controlled experimental design.\n- **Simulation-based calibration (SBC) validation** of posteriors is good practice and correctly deployed.\n\n### 5. Weaknesses\n\n- **The \"possibility frontier\" framing is post-hoc rationalization of a method that does not work well enough.** The paper's own results show: (a) 4PL+gamma is NOT significantly better than linear regression (p=0.18) or DCR (p=0.35), (b) RRF fusion (0.68) underperforms the best single signal (Embedding, 0.71), (c) per-benchmark gamma_k provides only 0.3pp improvement and fails to converge on HumanEval, (d) calibration degrades with model scale (MAE 5.8pp at 0.5B to 8.2pp at 7B), and (e) the method actively hurts in the RL regime (11.2pp vs 9.5pp uncalibrated). Reframing these failures as \"mapping the possibility frontier\" is creative but does not change the fact that the method does not deliver on its original promise.\n- **Insufficient novelty for a top venue.** The core model (4PL + covariate) is directly from the educational measurement literature. The Fisher Information analysis, while useful, is a relatively straightforward calculation. RRF is disclaimed as non-novel. The measurement error model is standard. What remains as genuinely new -- the signal survival heatmap and the identifiability threshold -- could be a workshop paper or a short paper, not a full submission.\n- **The causal inference analogy is superficial.** The paper repeatedly invokes the parallel to observational causal inference but never formalizes it. There is no DAG, no discussion of the assumptions required for the contamination covariate to satisfy unconfoundedness, no sensitivity analysis for unmeasured confounding. If the analogy is central to the framing, it needs to be formalized; if it is just motivation, it should not be repeated so prominently.\n- **22 references is thin.** For AAAI, 30-40 is typical for a methods paper with this scope. Key foundational works in IRT, causal inference, membership inference, and contamination detection are missing (see Section 3 above).\n- **Contradictory results across dream versions.** The \"best\" dream shows MAE=1.5pp with 93% coverage; the \"worst\" shows MAE=6.8pp with the method not significantly outperforming linear regression. The actual experiments (exp_004 running, exp_001 preparing) have not been completed. This means the paper's results are entirely simulated/projected, casting doubt on which version of reality will materialize.\n- **Single model family for controlled experiments.** Qwen2.5 only (in the worst-case/prompt version). The multi-family validation uses a single seed with 3.3pp MAE gap. This is insufficient to claim generalizability.\n- **HumanEval convergence failure is a red flag.** If per-benchmark gamma_k does not converge on HumanEval (N=164 items), this suggests the 4PL model is over-parameterized for small benchmarks -- a fundamental limitation that is acknowledged but not resolved.\n- **The \"calibration paradox\" undermines the practical value.** If calibration gets worse precisely for the models where users most need it (larger, RL-trained), the framework's utility is severely limited. The paper acknowledges this honestly but the implication is that ContamCal is useful only for a narrow slice of the evaluation landscape (small SFT models on large benchmarks) that is arguably the least concerning for contamination.\n\n### 6. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On the Fisher Information threshold:** The AUC threshold of 0.72 is derived under specific model assumptions (4PL with additive gamma). How sensitive is this threshold to model misspecification? If the true contamination effect is multiplicative rather than additive, or if there are interaction effects between contamination and item difficulty, does the threshold change substantially? Have you conducted sensitivity analysis on the functional form?\n\n2. **On the causal inference analogy:** You repeatedly frame ContamCal as analogous to observational causal inference. Can you formalize the assumptions required for the contamination covariate to identify the causal effect of contamination on scores? Specifically, what is the analog of the \"no unmeasured confounders\" assumption, and is it plausible in your setting? (Training regime, data curation practices, and model architecture all potentially confound the exposure-score relationship.)\n\n3. **On practical deployment:** Given that ContamCal works well only under SFT contamination of small models with high-quality detection signals, and that the models most commonly evaluated at scale are large RL-trained models -- who is the target user of this framework? Can you provide a concrete deployment scenario where ContamCal would change a practitioner's decision?\n\n4. **On the Self-Critique citation:** Different versions of your manuscript cite Self-Critique differently (Wust et al. 2025 vs. Wentzel et al. 2024), and the arXiv ID appears non-standard. Is this a real published work, an in-submission concurrent work, or your own contribution? If it is your own, this needs to be disclosed.\n\n5. **On identifiability vs. the 4PL itself:** The identifiability challenges you document -- are they specific to the contamination parameter gamma, or do they partly reflect the well-known identifiability pathologies of the 4PL model itself (c and d parameters notoriously difficult to estimate with limited data)? Have you compared your identifiability bounds to those from a 2PL+gamma model to disentangle these effects?\n\n### 7. Originality Score: 2 (Fair)\n\nThe core model is an adaptation of existing item compromise IRT with standard Bayesian techniques. The Fisher Information identifiability analysis and signal survival heatmap are useful contributions but not sufficient to reach \"good\" originality. The \"possibility frontier\" framing is creative but masks methodological limitations.\n\n### 8. Significance Score: 2 (Fair)\n\nThe paper addresses an important problem (contamination calibration), but the negative findings -- while honest -- severely limit practical impact. The framework works only in narrow conditions, and the identifiability thresholds, while informative, primarily tell the community \"this is hard\" without providing a path forward. The signal survival analysis has moderate standalone significance.\n\n### 9. Clarity Score: 3 (Good)\n\nThe paper is well-written with clear mathematical exposition. The two-stage pipeline is easy to follow. The framing oscillates between \"our method works\" and \"our method reveals impossibility\" which creates narrative tension, but the technical presentation is sound. The related work section is well-organized by paradigm.\n\n### 10. Overall Recommendation: 4 (Weak Reject / Borderline Reject)\n\n**Justification:** The paper tackles an important problem and does so with intellectual honesty, which I appreciate. However, the core novelty is limited -- applying existing psychometric models (item compromise IRT) to a new domain (LLM evaluation) with standard Bayesian machinery. The empirical results reveal that the method does not outperform simple baselines (linear regression, DCR) with statistical significance, and actively degrades performance in the RL setting. The \"possibility frontier\" framing, while intellectually interesting, is essentially post-hoc repackaging of a method that failed to deliver its original promise. For AAAI 2027, I would expect either (a) a method that demonstrably works, or (b) a much deeper theoretical contribution explaining why the problem is fundamentally hard, with formal impossibility results rather than empirical non-significance. The current paper falls between these two stools. The signal survival analysis is the strongest standalone contribution but would be better suited to a focused empirical study than embedded in a methods paper whose method does not clearly work.\n\nA revision could strengthen the paper substantially by: (1) formalizing the causal inference assumptions, (2) providing proper impossibility theorems rather than empirical observations, (3) expanding the reference list to 35+, (4) resolving the Self-Critique citation issue, and (5) narrowing the claims to match the demonstrated capabilities.\n\n### 11. Confidence: 4 (High)\n\nI have expertise in IRT models, Bayesian inference, and LLM evaluation. I am less familiar with the specific contamination detection methods (Min-K%++, ConStat) but have sufficient knowledge of the detection paradigm to evaluate the paper's claims. I have carefully examined all available versions of the manuscript and the project internals."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a72f0623630261e6f (use SendMessage with to: 'a72f0623630261e6f' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 68580\ntool_uses: 23\nduration_ms: 197068</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"96df0b3b-89a3-4c1d-a37a-4bedcebb08c8","uuid":"ac577a56-315d-409f-98c6-2f49ac390e4d","timestamp":"2026-05-13T18:15:15.553Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an AAAI 2027 submission. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## AAAI Review Scale (1-10)\n- 10: Award quality\n- 8-9: Strong accept\n- 7: Accept  \n- 6: Weak accept / borderline accept\n- 5: Borderline\n- 4: Weak reject / borderline reject\n- 3: Reject\n- 1-2: Strong reject\n\nEvaluate on: Novelty, Originality, Positioning relative to prior work, Significance of contributions, Clarity.\n\n## Paper: \"ContamCal: Mapping the Possibility Frontier of Contamination-Aware Benchmark Calibration\"\n\n### Paper Summary\nContamCal augments Item Response Theory (IRT) with contamination covariates. It fuses four contamination detection signals (Embedding Retrieval, Min-K%++, ConStat, Self-Critique) via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) into a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with Bayesian credible intervals. The paper's central contribution is establishing a \"possibility frontier\" for contamination calibration: practical identifiability requires detection signal AUC ≥ 0.72, which current signals meet only in narrow conditions (SFT regime, ≤1.5B models).\n\n### Three Claimed Contributions\n1. **Theoretical:** First Fisher Information identifiability analysis for contamination calibration, yielding AUC threshold of 0.72 and Cramér-Rao lower bounds for γ.\n2. **Empirical:** Signal survival analysis across four training regimes (Base, SFT-Clean, SFT-Contam, RL) showing gradual, benchmark-dependent degradation.\n3. **Methodological:** The ContamCal framework itself, providing calibration when signals are sufficient and honestly reporting high uncertainty when not.\n\n### Related Work (Section 2)\nThe paper cites and positions against:\n- **Contamination Detection:** Min-K%++ (Shi et al., 2024), ConStat (Dekoninck et al., 2024), Self-Critique (mentioned but no citation), CDD/KDS (Dong et al., 2024). Claims these flag but don't calibrate.\n- **IRT for LLM Evaluation:** Lalor et al. (2024) at AAAI 2024, Hi-BayES (Polo et al., 2024), ATLAS (Sedoc et al., 2020), Growing Pains (Vania et al., 2021). Claims none incorporate contamination covariates.\n- **Contamination Mitigation:** Xu et al. (2024) testing 20 strategies finding none effective, DCR (Yang et al., 2023b, EMNLP 2025), LiveBench (White et al., 2024). Claims DCR lacks uncertainty bounds and RL handling.\n- **Item Compromise IRT:** Eckerly (2017), Sinharay (2017) in educational measurement. This is the theoretical foundation.\n\n### Key Method (Section 3)\n- RRF fusion of 4 signals (design choice, not contribution)\n- 4PL IRT + contamination covariate γ: P(Y_ij=1 | θ_j, τ_i) = c_i + (d_i - c_i) · σ(a_i(θ_j + γ·τ_i - b_i))\n- Per-benchmark γ_k variant\n- Measurement error model for noisy exposure scores\n- Draws analogy to causal inference (confounders → covariates)\n\n### Key Empirical Findings\n- 4PL+γ MAE=6.8pp, NOT significantly better than linear regression (6.2pp, p=0.18) or DCR (6.4pp, p=0.35)\n- RRF fusion (0.68) underperforms best single signal (Embedding, 0.71)\n- Per-benchmark γ_k: negligible 0.3pp improvement, HumanEval doesn't converge\n- Calibration paradox: difficulty increases with model scale (MAE: 5.8pp→8.2pp from 0.5B→7B)\n- 72B observational: all signals near random (0.58), calibration MAE=11.8pp (worse than uncalibrated)\n- RL regime: calibration actively hurts (11.2pp vs 9.5pp uncalibrated, negative expected regret)\n- Signal survival: gradual degradation, not cliff-like; SFT-Clean vs SFT-Contam gap only 0.02 AUC\n- Closed-weight 2-signal: MAE=9.8pp, Kendall τ=0.52 (near random), worse than DCR\n- Multi-family (Llama vs Qwen): 3.3pp MAE gap, single seed, different inflation patterns\n\n### Reference List (22 references total)\nAdams et al. 1997, Balloccu et al. 2024, Cormack et al. 2009, De Boeck & Wilson 2004, Dekoninck et al. 2024, Dong et al. 2024, Eckerly 2017, Lalor et al. 2024, Maris & Bechger 2009, Mislevy 1986, Oren et al. 2024, Polo et al. 2024, Sainz et al. 2023, San Martín et al. 2009, Sedoc et al. 2020, Shi et al. 2024, Sinharay 2017, Talts et al. 2018, Vania et al. 2021, White et al. 2024, Xu et al. 2024, Yang et al. 2023a, Yang et al. 2023b.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed R2 review focusing on:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Novelty Assessment** (What is genuinely new? What is incremental? What is known?)\n3. **Related Work Gaps** (Missing references, unfair comparisons, positioning issues)\n4. **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n5. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific)\n6. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n7. **Originality Score** (1-4: 1=poor, 2=fair, 3=good, 4=excellent)\n8. **Significance Score** (1-4)\n9. **Clarity Score** (1-4)\n10. **Overall Recommendation** (1-10 with justification)\n11. **Confidence** (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider:\n- Is the \"possibility frontier\" framing genuinely novel, or is it post-hoc rationalization of a method that doesn't work?\n- The causal inference analogy: does it hold up or is it superficial?\n- Are 22 references sufficient for a venue like AAAI? What's missing?\n- The paper claims a \"negative but informative\" result — is this sufficiently novel for AAAI acceptance?\n- How does this compare to simply running the best detection signal (Embedding) alone?\n- Is the item compromise IRT literature adequately engaged with?\n- Self-Critique is mentioned but appears to lack a citation — is this the authors' own method or an uncited prior work?\n","agentId":"a72f0623630261e6f","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the materials needed. Let me synthesize my review. I've examined three versions of the paper (best, median, worst dream reports), the actual paper draft (introduction, related work, method sections), the paper framing document, the project roadmap, and the decisions log. I notice a critical discrepancy: the paper summary provided in the prompt describes a \"negative result\" paper (the \"worst\" dream tier), while the actual paper draft and \"best\" dream report describe a paper where ContamCal works well (1.5pp MAE, 93% coverage). I will review the version described in the prompt -- the \"possibility frontier\" / negative-result framing -- as that is what was assigned.\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: ContamCal -- Novelty and Related Work Focus\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper proposes ContamCal, a two-stage framework that augments 4PL Item Response Theory with contamination covariates derived from fusing four detection signals via Reciprocal Rank Fusion. The central finding is a \"possibility frontier\": Fisher Information analysis yields an AUC threshold of 0.72, below which the contamination parameter becomes unidentifiable, and current detection signals meet this threshold only under narrow conditions (SFT regime, small models). The paper frames this as a \"negative but informative\" result, arguing that it characterizes impossibility regions for any post-hoc calibration method.\n\n### 2. Novelty Assessment\n\n**Genuinely new:**\n- The idea of formally characterizing identifiability bounds for contamination calibration via Fisher Information analysis is novel. No prior work has asked \"under what signal quality conditions is contamination calibration even feasible?\" This is a legitimate and useful question.\n- The signal survival heatmap (signal x regime AUC matrix) is empirically novel. While Wang et al. (2025) showed RL degrades detection signals generally, the systematic four-signal x four-regime matrix with cliff-vs-gradual characterization adds value.\n\n**Incremental:**\n- The 4PL+gamma IRT model is a direct application of item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) with continuous rather than binary exposure covariates. The three \"structural adaptations\" are presented as non-trivial, but (a) fitting IRT to non-independent examinees has been discussed extensively in the psychometric literature (Reise & Waller 2009, Embretson & Reise 2000), (b) continuous covariates are standard in explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004), and (c) \"regime-conditional ability\" is handled through the measurement error variance rather than through actual regime-specific parameters, making it a notational distinction rather than a modeling advance.\n- RRF fusion of detection signals is explicitly disclaimed as a contribution, yet it occupies substantial space in the method section. This is honest but raises the question of what the actual methodological contribution is beyond the IRT application.\n- The measurement error model (Eq. 3, treating observed exposure as noisy proxy for latent exposure) is standard Bayesian measurement error modeling -- not novel, though its application to this domain is sensible.\n\n**Known:**\n- The causal inference analogy (measuring and adjusting for confounders rather than eliminating them) has been invoked in numerous applied statistics papers and does not constitute novelty.\n- The finding that detection signals degrade under RL post-training is already documented by Wang et al. (2025) and Wust et al. (2025). The contribution here is the quantitative characterization, not the phenomenon.\n- The \"mitigation doesn't work\" motivation is entirely from Xu et al. (2025), cited appropriately but meaning the problem motivation is derivative.\n\n### 3. Related Work Gaps\n\n**Missing references (significant):**\n- **Membership inference literature beyond Min-K%++:** Carlini et al. (2021, \"Extracting Training Data from Large Language Models\"), Mattern et al. (2023), and Shi et al. (2024, \"Detecting Pretraining Data from Large Language Models\") are foundational membership inference works that should be cited. The paper cites \"Shi et al. 2024\" in the prompt summary but uses \"Duan et al. 2025\" and \"Zhang et al. 2024\" in different draft versions -- this inconsistency raises concerns about citation management.\n- **Benchmark contamination surveys:** Deng et al. (2024, \"Investigating Data Contamination in Modern Benchmarks for Large Language Models\"), Balloccu et al. (2024) appear in some draft versions but not all. A comprehensive survey citation is essential.\n- **IRT identifiability literature:** San Martin et al. (2015) on identifiability of multidimensional IRT, Maris & Bechger (2009) on 3PL identifiability -- the paper cites these but does not engage deeply with the identifiability theory that underpins the Fisher Information analysis. How does the Fisher Information bound relate to the known identifiability pathologies of 3PL/4PL models (e.g., the Maris & Bechger argument that 3PL is not identifiable without constraints)?\n- **Reise & Waller (2009)** on IRT applications outside educational measurement -- directly relevant to the \"independent examinees\" adaptation claim.\n- **Embretson & Reise (2000)** \"Item Response Theory for Psychologists\" -- foundational reference missing.\n- **Causal inference for measurement:** The paper draws an analogy to causal inference but cites only Rosenbaum & Rubin (1983). If this analogy is central to the framing, Pearl (2009), Hernan & Robins (2020), or at minimum Rubin (1974) should be engaged with. As currently presented, the analogy is gestural rather than formal.\n- **Contamination in other evaluation paradigms:** Oren et al. (2024, \"Proving Test Set Contamination in Black Box Language Models\") -- cited inconsistently across drafts. Jacovi et al. (2023), Li et al. (2024) on contamination in generation benchmarks.\n- **Recent IRT for LLMs:** Gu et al. (2026, PSN-IRT) and Zhuang et al. (2025, HiBayES) appear in the actual draft but not in the prompt's reference list of 22 citations. The actual draft has approximately 23 references, which remains thin for a venue like AAAI/EMNLP that typically expects 30-50 citations.\n\n**Unfair comparisons / positioning issues:**\n- The positioning table (Table 1 in the related work draft) marks ContamCal as having all four desirable properties (Detects, Calibrates, UQ, RL-robust) with checkmarks. But the paper's own results show that ContamCal's RL-robustness consists of producing wide, uninformative CIs. Claiming \"RL-robust\" when the method produces 11.8pp MAE (worse than uncalibrated) under RL is misleading. \"RL-honest\" would be more accurate, but that is a different property than \"RL-robust.\"\n- DCR is characterized as lacking \"uncertainty bounds and RL handling,\" but DCR was not designed for these settings. The comparison should acknowledge scope differences rather than treating missing features as deficiencies.\n- Self-Critique is used as a signal throughout the paper but the citation situation is unclear. Different drafts cite it as \"Wust et al. 2025\" or \"Wentzel et al. 2024\" -- which is it? The arXiv ID \"2406.12345\" in the best-case draft bibliography appears suspiciously round and may be fabricated.\n\n### 4. Strengths\n\n- **Intellectually honest framing of limitations.** The paper acknowledges that its method does not work under RL and at large scales, and frames this as an identifiability boundary rather than hiding it. This is uncommon and commendable in ML papers.\n- **Well-grounded in psychometric theory.** The connection to item compromise IRT (Eckerly, Sinharay) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson) is appropriate and demonstrates interdisciplinary sophistication. The three structural adaptations, while incremental, are correctly identified.\n- **Fisher Information analysis provides theoretical grounding.** Even if the AUC threshold is ultimately a simple calculation, formalizing the identifiability boundary is a genuine contribution that gives the community a concrete target for detection signal improvement.\n- **Measurement error model is principled.** Propagating Stage 1 uncertainty into Stage 2 posteriors is the correct Bayesian approach, and the coverage comparison (83% without vs. 93% with) demonstrates its importance.\n- **Signal survival analysis is empirically valuable.** The signal x regime heatmap, even if the finding (RL degrades signals) is partially known, provides the first systematic quantification with a controlled experimental design.\n- **Simulation-based calibration (SBC) validation** of posteriors is good practice and correctly deployed.\n\n### 5. Weaknesses\n\n- **The \"possibility frontier\" framing is post-hoc rationalization of a method that does not work well enough.** The paper's own results show: (a) 4PL+gamma is NOT significantly better than linear regression (p=0.18) or DCR (p=0.35), (b) RRF fusion (0.68) underperforms the best single signal (Embedding, 0.71), (c) per-benchmark gamma_k provides only 0.3pp improvement and fails to converge on HumanEval, (d) calibration degrades with model scale (MAE 5.8pp at 0.5B to 8.2pp at 7B), and (e) the method actively hurts in the RL regime (11.2pp vs 9.5pp uncalibrated). Reframing these failures as \"mapping the possibility frontier\" is creative but does not change the fact that the method does not deliver on its original promise.\n- **Insufficient novelty for a top venue.** The core model (4PL + covariate) is directly from the educational measurement literature. The Fisher Information analysis, while useful, is a relatively straightforward calculation. RRF is disclaimed as non-novel. The measurement error model is standard. What remains as genuinely new -- the signal survival heatmap and the identifiability threshold -- could be a workshop paper or a short paper, not a full submission.\n- **The causal inference analogy is superficial.** The paper repeatedly invokes the parallel to observational causal inference but never formalizes it. There is no DAG, no discussion of the assumptions required for the contamination covariate to satisfy unconfoundedness, no sensitivity analysis for unmeasured confounding. If the analogy is central to the framing, it needs to be formalized; if it is just motivation, it should not be repeated so prominently.\n- **22 references is thin.** For AAAI, 30-40 is typical for a methods paper with this scope. Key foundational works in IRT, causal inference, membership inference, and contamination detection are missing (see Section 3 above).\n- **Contradictory results across dream versions.** The \"best\" dream shows MAE=1.5pp with 93% coverage; the \"worst\" shows MAE=6.8pp with the method not significantly outperforming linear regression. The actual experiments (exp_004 running, exp_001 preparing) have not been completed. This means the paper's results are entirely simulated/projected, casting doubt on which version of reality will materialize.\n- **Single model family for controlled experiments.** Qwen2.5 only (in the worst-case/prompt version). The multi-family validation uses a single seed with 3.3pp MAE gap. This is insufficient to claim generalizability.\n- **HumanEval convergence failure is a red flag.** If per-benchmark gamma_k does not converge on HumanEval (N=164 items), this suggests the 4PL model is over-parameterized for small benchmarks -- a fundamental limitation that is acknowledged but not resolved.\n- **The \"calibration paradox\" undermines the practical value.** If calibration gets worse precisely for the models where users most need it (larger, RL-trained), the framework's utility is severely limited. The paper acknowledges this honestly but the implication is that ContamCal is useful only for a narrow slice of the evaluation landscape (small SFT models on large benchmarks) that is arguably the least concerning for contamination.\n\n### 6. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On the Fisher Information threshold:** The AUC threshold of 0.72 is derived under specific model assumptions (4PL with additive gamma). How sensitive is this threshold to model misspecification? If the true contamination effect is multiplicative rather than additive, or if there are interaction effects between contamination and item difficulty, does the threshold change substantially? Have you conducted sensitivity analysis on the functional form?\n\n2. **On the causal inference analogy:** You repeatedly frame ContamCal as analogous to observational causal inference. Can you formalize the assumptions required for the contamination covariate to identify the causal effect of contamination on scores? Specifically, what is the analog of the \"no unmeasured confounders\" assumption, and is it plausible in your setting? (Training regime, data curation practices, and model architecture all potentially confound the exposure-score relationship.)\n\n3. **On practical deployment:** Given that ContamCal works well only under SFT contamination of small models with high-quality detection signals, and that the models most commonly evaluated at scale are large RL-trained models -- who is the target user of this framework? Can you provide a concrete deployment scenario where ContamCal would change a practitioner's decision?\n\n4. **On the Self-Critique citation:** Different versions of your manuscript cite Self-Critique differently (Wust et al. 2025 vs. Wentzel et al. 2024), and the arXiv ID appears non-standard. Is this a real published work, an in-submission concurrent work, or your own contribution? If it is your own, this needs to be disclosed.\n\n5. **On identifiability vs. the 4PL itself:** The identifiability challenges you document -- are they specific to the contamination parameter gamma, or do they partly reflect the well-known identifiability pathologies of the 4PL model itself (c and d parameters notoriously difficult to estimate with limited data)? Have you compared your identifiability bounds to those from a 2PL+gamma model to disentangle these effects?\n\n### 7. Originality Score: 2 (Fair)\n\nThe core model is an adaptation of existing item compromise IRT with standard Bayesian techniques. The Fisher Information identifiability analysis and signal survival heatmap are useful contributions but not sufficient to reach \"good\" originality. The \"possibility frontier\" framing is creative but masks methodological limitations.\n\n### 8. Significance Score: 2 (Fair)\n\nThe paper addresses an important problem (contamination calibration), but the negative findings -- while honest -- severely limit practical impact. The framework works only in narrow conditions, and the identifiability thresholds, while informative, primarily tell the community \"this is hard\" without providing a path forward. The signal survival analysis has moderate standalone significance.\n\n### 9. Clarity Score: 3 (Good)\n\nThe paper is well-written with clear mathematical exposition. The two-stage pipeline is easy to follow. The framing oscillates between \"our method works\" and \"our method reveals impossibility\" which creates narrative tension, but the technical presentation is sound. The related work section is well-organized by paradigm.\n\n### 10. Overall Recommendation: 4 (Weak Reject / Borderline Reject)\n\n**Justification:** The paper tackles an important problem and does so with intellectual honesty, which I appreciate. However, the core novelty is limited -- applying existing psychometric models (item compromise IRT) to a new domain (LLM evaluation) with standard Bayesian machinery. The empirical results reveal that the method does not outperform simple baselines (linear regression, DCR) with statistical significance, and actively degrades performance in the RL setting. The \"possibility frontier\" framing, while intellectually interesting, is essentially post-hoc repackaging of a method that failed to deliver its original promise. For AAAI 2027, I would expect either (a) a method that demonstrably works, or (b) a much deeper theoretical contribution explaining why the problem is fundamentally hard, with formal impossibility results rather than empirical non-significance. The current paper falls between these two stools. The signal survival analysis is the strongest standalone contribution but would be better suited to a focused empirical study than embedded in a methods paper whose method does not clearly work.\n\nA revision could strengthen the paper substantially by: (1) formalizing the causal inference assumptions, (2) providing proper impossibility theorems rather than empirical observations, (3) expanding the reference list to 35+, (4) resolving the Self-Critique citation issue, and (5) narrowing the claims to match the demonstrated capabilities.\n\n### 11. Confidence: 4 (High)\n\nI have expertise in IRT models, Bayesian inference, and LLM evaluation. I am less familiar with the specific contamination detection methods (Min-K%++, ConStat) but have sufficient knowledge of the detection paradigm to evaluate the paper's claims. I have carefully examined all available versions of the manuscript and the project internals."}],"totalDurationMs":197068,"totalTokens":68580,"totalToolUseCount":23,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":684,"cache_read_input_tokens":63831,"output_tokens":4062,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":684},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":4062,"cache_read_input_tokens":63831,"cache_creation_input_tokens":684,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":684,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":7,"searchCount":5,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":11}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a9cc254f239305d2f","tool_use_id":"toolu_01StMkgFznUJ2jWkHjGWh7eb","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":118580,"tool_uses":32,"duration_ms":250025},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"66fde220-7605-45cf-8716-370b869ed02b","session_id":"96df0b3b-89a3-4c1d-a37a-4bedcebb08c8"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Q26kBMsHX4mjqVB3yF5ctf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TB46nTrsKWKDr1GHtinN8b","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"contamcal-irt-calibration","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260513T171200","overall_score":"4 — Weak Reject","report":"---\ndream_id: dream_20260513T171200\ntier: worst\noverall_score: 4 — Weak Reject\ntimestamp: 2026-05-13T19:45:00+00:00\n---\n\n# Dream Review Report — ContamCal: Mapping the Possibility Frontier of Contamination-Aware Benchmark Calibration\n\n**Dream ID**: 20260513T171200  \n**Reviewer**: R1 (Methods and Experiments)  \n**Target Venue**: AAAI 2027 (1-10 scale, ~17.6% acceptance rate)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nContamCal proposes a two-stage framework combining Reciprocal Rank Fusion of four contamination detection signals with a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model. The paper's central contribution is reframed as establishing a \"possibility frontier\" for contamination calibration via Fisher Information analysis, identifying a practical identifiability threshold at AUC >= 0.72 that current detection signals meet only under restricted conditions (small SFT models at high dosage). While the experimental coverage is comprehensive (12 planned experiments executed), the results systematically demonstrate that the proposed calibration method is not significantly better than simple baselines (linear regression, DCR), and in the RL regime actively degrades performance below uncalibrated scores.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n- **Thorough identifiability analysis with genuine theoretical contribution.** The Fisher Information derivation distinguishing structural identifiability (AUC >= 0.58) from practical identifiability (AUC >= 0.72, CR bound <= 0.15) is the paper's strongest intellectual contribution. The 144-condition simulation sweep (6 AUC x 4 N_models x 3 priors x 2 exposure types, 10 seeds) provides systematic empirical validation of the phase transition at AUC ~ 0.70 where gamma RMSE drops from 0.31 to 0.19 and coverage jumps from 74% to 82%. This has genuine prescriptive value for the detection signal community.\n\n- **Honest and systematic negative reporting.** The paper is unusual in its willingness to present results where the method fails. Tables 10, 12, 18, and 19 all show conditions where ContamCal performs comparably to or worse than simpler methods. The RL case study (Table 18: MAE=11.2pp vs uncalibrated 9.5pp, negative expected regret) is particularly forthright. This intellectual honesty is commendable.\n\n- **Comprehensive experimental design spanning the relevant parameter space.** The controlled SFT injection (15 runs, 3 scales, 5 dosages, 4 benchmarks) provides ground truth. The 7 baselines, signal survival heatmap, noise propagation analysis, scale bridging, multi-family validation, and closed-weight ablation collectively address a wide range of reviewer concerns pre-emptively.\n\n- **Signal survival analysis has independent empirical value.** The signal x regime AUC heatmap (Table 15) is a genuinely novel contribution regardless of the calibration outcome. The finding that degradation is gradual (~0.035/step) rather than cliff-like, and that the best signal varies by benchmark under RL, refutes implicit assumptions in the detection community.\n\n- **Sound Bayesian modeling infrastructure.** The Stan code (4PL+gamma with measurement error, reviewed in contam_irt_4pl_me.stan) is properly structured. The generated quantities block correctly computes calibrated scores by setting exposure=0, the measurement error model correctly specifies x ~ N(tau, sigma_e^2), and convergence diagnostics (R-hat, ESS, divergences) are systematically checked.\n\n- **Anti-circularity argument for RRF is well-reasoned.** The empirical evidence (calibrated weighted RRF AUC=0.69 vs vanilla RRF 0.68, not significant at p>0.10) supports the claim that learned aggregation weights are not worth the meta-circularity risk.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n- **(W1) Core calibration method fails to significantly outperform simple baselines (Table 10).** 4PL+gamma MAE=6.8pp is NOT significantly better than linear regression (6.2pp, p=0.18) or DCR (6.4pp, p=0.35). Only vanilla 4PL and Emperor mitigation are significantly beaten (p=0.02 and p=0.04), but these are strawmen — vanilla 4PL ignores contamination entirely and Emperor is known to fail (Xu et al. 2024). When the proposed method with 5 parameters per item plus gamma cannot beat a linear regression with ~3 parameters total, the added complexity is not justified by the data. This is the paper's fundamental problem: a sophisticated Bayesian IRT model is empirically dominated by OLS.\n\n- **(W2) RRF aggregation DEGRADES signal quality relative to the best single signal (Tables 7-8).** 4-signal RRF AUC=0.68 is 3pp WORSE than embedding alone (0.71). This is a serious methodological embarrassment for the Stage 1 pipeline. The root cause is pairwise signal correlation r_bar=0.55 — signals share too much variance for fusion to add information. When the aggregate is worse than the best component, the aggregation step is actively harmful. The meta-circularity argument against learned weights is moot when the unsupervised alternative actively degrades the input.\n\n- **(W3) Calibration HURTS in the RL regime (Table 18).** 4PL+gamma MAE=11.2pp vs uncalibrated 9.5pp — the method makes things worse. Expected regret is negative (-0.05), meaning ContamCal degrades model selection decisions. 88% of gamma CIs include zero. This is not merely a limitation — it means deploying ContamCal on RL-trained models (i.e., essentially all production models) would produce worse evaluation outcomes than doing nothing.\n\n- **(W4) Systematic undercoverage destroys the UQ claim.** Credible intervals achieve 76% coverage at the 95% nominal level and 58% at 80% nominal (Table 10). This is not a small miscalibration — the 19pp gap at the 95% level means the intervals are fundamentally unreliable as uncertainty quantification. The measurement error model achieves 92% coverage (Table 9) but only by inflating CI width to 22pp — vacuously wide intervals that cover most outcomes trivially. 72% of conditions exceed the 15pp CI width threshold. A 95% CI that says \"true score is between 38% and 82%\" provides no actionable information.\n\n- **(W5) gamma-theta posterior confounding (r=0.63, Table 12) indicates model misspecification.** The high posterior correlation between ability and contamination effect means the model cannot reliably distinguish \"model is good\" from \"model memorized the test.\" At AUC=0.68 (the achieved signal quality), the simulation sweep shows this confounding is expected (Table: theta-gamma posterior correlation = 0.58 at AUC=0.65, rising to 0.68 at 7B scale). This is not a sampling issue but a fundamental identifiability problem given available signal quality. The additive gamma specification on the logit scale compounds this: contamination effect is assumed constant across items of all difficulties, which the benchmark-dependent gamma_k variation (0.18 to 0.42) and dose-response non-monotonicity directly contradict.\n\n- **(W6) Per-benchmark gamma_k provides negligible improvement and fails on HumanEval.** The per-benchmark variant yields only 0.3pp MAE improvement over global gamma (6.5 vs 6.8, Table 10). HumanEval does not converge (ESS=85, R-hat=1.12) with N=164 items. The gamma_k CIs overlap 78% on average, and a Wald test shows no significant difference from global gamma. The paper claims heterogeneous benchmark susceptibility as a contribution, but the evidence is too weak to support it — the model lacks statistical power to detect the differences.\n\n### Moderate\n\n- **(W7) Scale-dependent degradation makes the method least useful where most needed (Tables 13-14).** At 7B: MAE=8.2pp (worse than DCR 7.0pp), gamma-theta correlation=0.68. At 72B: best signal AUC=0.58, MAE=11.8pp (worse than uncalibrated), CI width=27pp. The trend is monotonically worsening: as models grow (and contamination scrutiny increases), calibration quality systematically degrades. The paper's identifiability threshold is crossed between 1.5B and 3B — all practically relevant models fall below it.\n\n- **(W8) Multi-family validation is critically underpowered (Table 19).** Single seed, 3 dosage levels only. The 3.3pp MAE gap between Llama-1B and Qwen-1.5B is large relative to within-family variance (<1.5pp) but evaluated with n=1. Signal AUC drops 0.10 (Qwen: 0.72, Llama: 0.62). Gamma CI includes zero for Llama even at 100% dosage. This is insufficient to claim any cross-family generalizability and suggests the method's performance is highly architecture-dependent.\n\n- **(W9) Dose-response is non-monotonic, undermining the causal interpretation.** Gamma CI includes zero at 5% and 25% dosage (Table 12). Spearman rho=0.58 (Table 12) is far from the 0.95+ expected for monotonic dose-response. At 5% dosage, inflation is 0.9pp with std=2.9pp — completely indistinguishable from noise. The \"marginal memorization return\" explanation for the 25-to-50% plateau is post-hoc and untested.\n\n- **(W10) 2-signal closed-weight mode is worse than DCR (Table 20).** MAE=9.8pp (+44% vs 4-signal), Kendall tau=0.52 (near random), CI width=22pp (+132%). DCR achieves 8.5pp on the same conditions. For closed-weight models (GPT-4, Claude) — the models under most contamination scrutiny — ContamCal's available configuration is strictly dominated by a simpler existing method.\n\n- **(W11) Local independence violation is acknowledged but not formally analyzed.** Posterior predictive checks show 35% of item pairs have residual correlations exceeding 0.05 (Section on convergence diagnostics). The paper attributes the coverage gap partly to this violation but provides no formal analysis of expected coverage degradation under the observed correlation structure. The Mislevy (1986) reference is cited but the implications are not worked through.\n\n### Minor\n\n- **(W12) No principled model selection between global gamma and per-benchmark gamma_k.** WAIC and LOO-CV are not reported. The generated quantities block computes log_lik for LOO-CV but the paper does not use it.\n\n- **(W13) SFT injection as contamination proxy is not validated against in-the-wild contamination.** The gap between SFT-clean and SFT-contaminated signal AUC is only 0.02 (Table 15), suggesting signals partly detect \"SFT happened\" rather than \"contamination happened.\"\n\n- **(W14) RRF k=60 is not justified.** K-sensitivity analysis (Table 7) shows the range k=20-100, but the choice of k=60 default from information retrieval literature is adopted without domain-specific justification.\n\n- **(W15) Computational cost is not reported end-to-end.** Individual NUTS fit takes 35 min/benchmark for MMLU, but total pipeline cost (4 signals + RRF + IRT fitting) is unclear.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On W1**: Given that linear regression (MAE=6.2pp) beats 4PL+gamma (MAE=6.8pp), what is the concrete practical scenario where a user should prefer the IRT-based method? The UQ advantage is undermined by systematic undercoverage (76% at 95% nominal). Is there a simpler model (e.g., 2PL+gamma with bootstrap CIs) that achieves comparable MAE with better-calibrated intervals?\n\n2. **On W2**: Have you tried using only the best single signal (embedding) as Stage 1 output, bypassing RRF entirely? Given that RRF degrades AUC by 0.03 relative to embedding alone, the fusion step appears harmful. What is the argument for keeping it beyond the meta-circularity reasoning (which applies to learned weights, not to using the best available signal)?\n\n3. **On W3**: The paper frames wide CIs in the RL regime as \"correct behavior.\" But the expected regret is negative — meaning users are actively harmed by following ContamCal's recommendations. How do you reconcile \"correct uncertainty\" with \"worse decisions\"? Should the paper include a formal recommendation against deploying in regimes where signal AUC < 0.60?\n\n4. **On W5**: The gamma-theta posterior correlation of 0.63 exceeds the 0.58 value at AUC=0.65 in the simulation sweep, suggesting the real-data identifiability is even worse than simulated. Have you explored reparameterizations (e.g., modeling theta_contaminated = theta_clean + f(exposure) rather than additive gamma on the logit scale) that might reduce this confounding?\n\n5. **On W9**: The 5% dosage inflation of 0.9pp is within noise (std=2.9pp). For realistic contamination scenarios (widely believed to be in the 1-10% range for major benchmarks), does this mean ContamCal has zero sensitivity to the contamination levels of practical interest?\n\n---\n\n## 5. Minor Issues\n\n- Table numbering in the paper summary (Tables 4-20) should be verified against actual manuscript — the review context references may not match final table numbers.\n- The phrase \"possibility frontier\" is borrowed from economics (production possibility frontier) but used differently — clarify the analogy or choose a different term.\n- The paper should disambiguate \"calibration\" from probability calibration (Guo et al. 2017) early in the introduction.\n- Signal computation details (embedding model, Min-K% hyperparameters, ConStat configuration) should be in the main paper, not just appendix, for reproducibility.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Soundness Score: 2/4 (Fair)\n\nThe theoretical framework (Fisher Information analysis, identifiability bounds) is sound and well-executed. However, the core empirical claim — that 4PL+gamma provides meaningful calibration improvement — is not supported by the data. The method does not significantly beat simple baselines, exhibits systematic undercoverage, and actively hurts in the RL regime. The measurement error model achieves coverage at the cost of vacuous intervals. The gamma-theta confounding at achieved signal quality levels means the model's key parameter is not reliably identified in practice. The gap between the theoretical analysis (which is solid) and the empirical performance (which is negative) is the central soundness concern.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Significance Score: 2/4 (Fair)\n\nThe identifiability analysis and signal survival mapping have genuine value as diagnostic tools for the community. The \"possibility frontier\" framing — establishing when calibration is and isn't feasible — is informative. However, the paper does not deliver a working calibration method for practically relevant scenarios (models >= 7B, RL-trained, closed-weight). The method's viable operating region (small open-weight SFT models at high contamination dosage) is precisely the regime of least practical interest, where contamination is easily detected by simpler means and the models themselves are rarely used in high-stakes evaluations.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Clarity Score: 3/4 (Good)\n\nThe paper is well-organized with comprehensive experimental coverage. The honest reporting of negative results is commendable. The theoretical analysis is clearly presented. The reframing as \"possibility frontier\" rather than \"working calibration method\" is appropriate given the results. Weaknesses in clarity: (a) the distinction between \"calibration\" in the decontamination sense vs. probability calibration is not made early enough, (b) the number of tables (20) is overwhelming — consider consolidating, (c) the paper would benefit from a clear \"when to use / when not to use\" decision flowchart based on the identifiability analysis.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Overall Recommendation: 4/10 — Weak Reject\n\n**Justification:** The paper presents a principled framework with strong theoretical grounding, but the empirical results are fundamentally negative for the calibration claim. Specifically:\n\n(a) The method does not significantly beat linear regression (p=0.18) or DCR (p=0.35) — the two most relevant baselines.\n(b) RRF fusion is worse than the best single signal (-0.03 AUC).\n(c) Calibration actively hurts in the RL regime (MAE 11.2pp vs 9.5pp uncalibrated).\n(d) Credible intervals are systematically miscalibrated (76% at 95% nominal) or vacuously wide (22pp).\n(e) The method's viable operating region (small SFT models, high dosage, open-weight) is of limited practical interest.\n\nThe identifiability analysis and signal survival mapping are genuine contributions, but they are supporting analyses — without a working calibration method, they are diagnostic tools searching for a viable application. The paper would be substantially strengthened by either: (i) finding a regime where calibration demonstrably works well (e.g., using much better detection signals, or restricting to MMLU-only where signal quality is highest), or (ii) fully committing to the negative-result framing and providing formal impossibility bounds rather than a method that doesn't work.\n\nFor AAAI at 17.6% acceptance, the combination of negative core results, marginal statistical evidence, and limited practical applicability places this below the threshold. The work is not without merit — the identifiability analysis and signal survival analysis could anchor a revised submission — but the current framing overreaches relative to what the evidence supports.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Confidence: 4/5 (High)\n\nI have expertise in Bayesian modeling, IRT, and LLM evaluation. I reviewed the Stan code, the experimental scripts, and the simulation design in detail. I am confident in the assessment that the gamma-theta confounding at current signal AUC levels is a fundamental barrier, not a tuning issue. My uncertainty is mainly around whether improved detection signals (AUC >= 0.80) could unlock the method — but this is speculative and not demonstrated in the paper.\n\n---\n\n## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\n| Experiment | Plan ID | Status | Coverage |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Controlled SFT injection | exp-001 | Complete | Full: 15 runs, 3 scales, 5 dosages, 4 benchmarks |\n| Stage 1 signal pipeline | exp-002 | Complete | Full: 4 signals, all models |\n| RRF aggregation ablation | exp-003 | Complete | Full: 8+ aggregation variants |\n| Simulation identifiability sweep | exp-004 | Complete | Full: 144 conditions x 10 seeds |\n| Noise propagation analysis | exp-005 | Complete | Full: ME model + post-hoc injection |\n| IRT calibration + baselines | exp-006 | Complete | Full: 4PL+gamma (2 variants) + 7 baselines |\n| Scale bridging 7B | exp-007 | Complete | Full: 5 dosages, controlled SFT |\n| Signal survival analysis | exp-008 | Complete | Full: 4 signals x 4 regimes |\n| RL observational case study | exp-009 | Complete | Full: 8 models, regret analysis |\n| Multi-family (Llama) | exp-014 | Partial | 3 dosages, 1 seed (GPU budget constraint) |\n| 72B observational | exp-012 | Complete | Full: 2-signal mode, observational only |\n| 2-signal closed-weight | exp-013 | Complete | Full: degradation quantified |\n\n**Coverage: 12/12 experiments present, 11 full + 1 partial (exp-014).** The partial multi-family result (single seed) is a notable limitation for generalizability claims."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32194,"cache_read_input_tokens":85424,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":32194,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01StMkgFznUJ2jWkHjGWh7eb","session_id":"96df0b3b-89a3-4c1d-a37a-4bedcebb08c8","uuid":"c21ed305-e29d-47ce-a86c-7e2bbed91d08"}
